<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Montevideo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Montevideo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>, on the northern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>. Founded by the Spanish in the 1720s as a fortress against Portuguese expansion, it became the main naval base of the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata. It was fought over by royalists, Artigas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, Portugal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> before becoming the capital of independent Uruguay in 1828–1830.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/montevideo.jpg" alt="Montevideo" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eduardo Ruggieri · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PALACIO_LEGISLATIVO_01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain founded Soriano in 1624, Portugal a fort at Colonia del Sacramento in 1680, and Spain <b>Montevideo</b> in 1726 as a stronghold whose harbour soon rivalled Buenos Aires.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oriental-republic-of-uruguay">Oriental Republic of Uruguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Córdoba's counter-revolution under the former viceroy Santiago de Liniers was crushed and he was shot, but Paraguay and <b>Montevideo</b> swore to the Regency and the Viceroyalty of Peru annexed Upper Peru, so the Junta sent armies against all three: the beginning of the Argentine War of Independence, before…</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-provinces-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">United Provinces of the Río de la Plata · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Damaged, she took refuge in <b>Montevideo</b>, and four days later her captain scuttled her.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-13">13 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese forces from Brazil invaded the Banda Oriental in 1816 to crush José Artigas, and General Carlos Frederico Lecor entered <b>Montevideo</b> in January 1817, ending the Eastern Province.</blockquote><a href="/state/cisplatina">Cisplatina</a></li><li><blockquote>Its presence provoked Spain to found <b>Montevideo</b> in 1724.</blockquote><a href="/state/colonia-del-sacramento">Colonia del Sacramento</a></li><li><blockquote>The Banda Oriental, east of the Uruguay river, rose against Spanish <b>Montevideo</b> in 1811 under José Gervasio Artigas.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-province">Eastern Province</a></li></ul></section>
